<article class="env-proposition" id="CE70EF">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/CE70EF">Proposition <span data-tag="CE70EF">3.1.5.2</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a simplicial set. There exists a left proper, combinatorial model structure on the category $(\mSet )_{/S}$ which may be described as follows: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(C)$"><p>
A map $f: (X, \calE _{X}) \rightarrow (Y, \calE _ Y)$ is a cofibration if and only if the underlying map $X \rightarrow Y$ is a monomorphism of simplicial sets. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(W)$"><p>
A map $f: (X, \calE _{X}) \rightarrow (Y, \calE _{Y})$ is a weak equivalence if and only if the induced map $X^{\sharp } \rightarrow Y^{\sharp }$ is a Cartesian equivalence in $(\mSet )_{/S}$. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(F)$"><p>
A map $f: (X, \calE _{X}) \rightarrow (Y, \calE _ Y)$ is a fibration if and only if it has the right lifting property with respect to all trivial cofibrations. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      It suffices to show that the conditions of Proposition <a href="/tag/8C7DD0" data-tag="8C7DD0">8.2.6.13</a> are satisfied. We check them in turn: 
    <p>
      <ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="(1)"><p>
The class $(W)$ of Cartesian equivalences is perfect, in the sense of Definition <a href="/tag/7E81CB" data-tag="7E81CB">8.2.6.10</a>. This follows from Corollary <a href="/tag/23E4EE" data-tag="23E4EE">8.2.6.12</a>, since the class of Cartesian equivalences is perfect, and the functor $(X, \calE _ X) \rightarrow X^{\sharp }$ commutes with filtered colimits. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(2)"><p>
The class of weak equivalences is stable under pushouts by cofibrations. This follows from the analogous property of the Cartesian model structure, since the functor $(X, \calE _ X) \mapsto X^{\sharp }$ preserves pushouts. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(3)"><p>
A map $p: (X,\calE _ X) \rightarrow (Y,\calE _ Y)$ which has the right lifting property with respect to every cofibration is a weak equivalence. In this case, the underlying map of simplicial sets is a trivial fibration, so the induced map $X^{\sharp } \rightarrow Y^{\sharp }$ has the right lifting property with respect to all trivial cofibrations, and is a Cartesian equivalence as observed in the proof of Proposition <a href="/tag/A2E141" data-tag="A2E141">3.1.3.7</a>. 
</p>
</li>
</ul>
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>